        { q: 'Does HomeAdvisor sell your contact info for concrete project requests?', a: 'Yes. HomeAdvisor (now merged with Angi) distributes your project request to typically 3–5 contractors immediately after you submit a form. Each contractor pays for the lead, so multiple companies receive your contact information at once. For high-dollar concrete projects, this often results in aggressive follow-up from contractors who have never seen your property.' },
        { q: 'What license does a concrete contractor need in California?', a: "Concrete contractors in California must hold a C-8 Concrete Contractor specialty license issued by the California Contractors State License Board (CSLB). You can verify any contractor's C-8 license status at cslb.ca.gov. Always confirm that the license is current and that the contractor carries both general liability insurance and workers' compensation coverage." },
        { q: 'Do I need a permit for a concrete driveway in Murrieta, CA?', a: 'In most cases, yes. The City of Murrieta and Riverside County require permits for new driveway construction, major driveway replacements, and some patio additions. A legitimate licensed contractor will address permit requirements during the estimate and include permit costs in their proposal. Be cautious of contractors who suggest skipping permits — unpermitted concrete work can create issues when you sell your home.' },
        { q: 'Why does concrete crack in Murrieta?', a: "Murrieta sits on expansive clay soil that swells when wet and contracts in dry heat. Without proper base preparation — typically 4–6 inches of compacted aggregate base — and correctly spaced expansion joints, concrete slabs will crack as the soil moves seasonally. A contractor familiar with Murrieta's soil conditions will spec the base depth and joint spacing accordingly." },
        { q: 'What is the best way to find a concrete contractor in Murrieta without HomeAdvisor?', a: "Search \"concrete contractor Murrieta\" on Google and call businesses from the organic results directly. Then verify each contractor's C-8 license at the CSLB website before scheduling a site visit. Ask specifically about base preparation for expansive clay soil, permit handling, and whether they have experience working with local HOAs. Require a free written on-site estimate before committing." },
